{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI never expected a single week to feel so full. Japan surprised me with quiet beauty and electric energy—often in the same breath.\n\nIn Kyoto, I wandered through Fushimi Inari at dawn, walking beneath thousands of vermilion torii gates, their color glowing like fire against the mist. No crowds. Just the rustle of leaves and distant temple bells. Later, I sipped matcha in a centuries-old tea house, the bitterness balanced by a single sweet wagashi—each bite a meditation.\n\nTokyo was a different rhythm. Shibuya’s scramble crossing felt like a human tide, yet within minutes, I slipped into a tucked-away izakaya where the chef grilled yakitori with quiet precision. No menu—just “omakase.” He smiled, nodded, and served me chicken skin, liver, and a slice of grilled peach glazed with soy. I ate in silence, savoring every flavor.\n\nIn Kanazawa, I touched the gold leaf on Kenrokuen Garden’s lanterns, then watched artisans hand-paint washi paper in a workshop so still, I could hear the brush whisper against rice paper. At night, I rode the bullet train to Hiroshima, where the Peace Memorial Park hummed with reverence. A single origami crane, left by a visitor, rested on the memorial’s edge—I left mine beside it.\n\nJapan doesn’t shout. It invites. You lean in. You pause. You notice the way steam curls from a ramen bowl, how a street vendor bows without speaking, how even the vending machine lights glow with intention.\n\nI came for the temples and tech. I left with stillness.\n\nI didn’t buy much. But I carried home the quiet: the scent of tatami, the sound of rain on a tiled roof, the warmth of a stranger’s “arigatou” whispered like a secret.\n\nJapan didn’t just show me a country. It reminded me how to be present.\n\n— 298 words", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logpro